        "topic": "Unknown Topic",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "question": "What is present between gap junctions?",
        "options": {
            "A": "Connexin",
            "B": "Protein layer",
            "C": "Cellular adhesion",
            "D": "Lipid bilayer",
            "E": ""
        },
        "correctAnswer": "A",
        "explanation": "<p><h3 style=\"color: black;\">Correct Answer:</h3></p><p><p><strong style=\"color: #008000;\">Connexin</strong> - Gap junctions are formed by channel proteins called <strong>connexons</strong>, and each connexon is made of six <strong>connexin</strong> protein subunits. Connexons of adjacent cells dock together to form the intercellular channel.</p></p><p><br></p><p><h3 style=\"color: black;\">How to Pick the Answer:</h3></p><p><p>The key clue is <strong>gap junction</strong>. Gap junctions are protein channels that directly connect the cytoplasm of adjacent cells. The protein subunits forming these channels are <strong>connexins</strong>.</p></p><p><br></p><p><h3 style=\"color: black;\">Wrong Answers</h3></p><p><p><strong style=\"color: #CC0000;\">Protein layer</strong> - Gap junctions are made of specific channel proteins, not a nonspecific protein layer.</p></p><p><br></p><p><p><strong style=\"color: #CC0000;\">Cellular adhesion</strong> - Cellular adhesion is associated with junctions such as desmosomes and adherens junctions, rather than being the channel component of gap junctions.</p></p><p><br></p><p><p><strong style=\"color: #CC0000;\">Lipid bilayer</strong> - The cell membrane contains a lipid bilayer, but it does not form the communicating channel between cells.</p></p><p><br></p><p><h3 style=\"color: #0066CC;\">Key Concept:</h3></p><p><p><strong>Connexin → Connexon → Gap junction.</strong> Six connexin proteins assemble to form one connexon. Two connexons, one from each adjacent cell, join to create the gap-junction channel.</p></p><p><br></p><p><h3 style=\"color: #8A2BE2;\">Mnemonic:</h3></p><p><p><strong>CONNEXin = CONNEXion</strong> between cells.</p></p>",
        "hint": "<p><br></p>"
    },
